    w3_lore_title:
      "🪐 Plain-English Story (No Jargon): What Is a 'Manifold', Who Are Calabi & Yau, and Why Does Science Care?",
    w3_lore_lead:
      "Looking at the glowing 4D torus above, you might wonder if it is just sci-fi computer art. In reality, you are looking at one of the most famous geometric shapes in modern science. Here is the plain-English story of what it is, the 23-year mystery behind it, and how it connects school polynomials to the fabric of our Universe.",
    w3_lore_c1:
      "<div class='lore-title'>1. What Is a 'Manifold' in Plain English?</div>Imagine a tiny ant walking across a giant basketball or a donut. Right around its feet, the ant sees a flat floor and thinks the world is a flat 2D sheet. Yet if it walks straight ahead long enough, it loops all the way around and returns to its starting point! Any smooth geometric surface with no sharp corners or jagged tears is called a <b>Manifold</b>. And when that smooth shape is carved out of space by a single polynomial equation — such as <b>z₁⁵ + z₂⁵ = c</b> — mathematicians call it an <b>algebraic manifold</b>. Your school polynomial acts like a 4D laser sculptor carving a multi-petal crystal out of four-dimensional space!",
    w3_lore_c2:
      "<div class='lore-title'>2. Who Were Eugenio Calabi & Shing-Tung Yau? (A 23-Year Detective Story: 1954 → 1977)</div><ul><li><b>Eugenio Calabi</b> <i>(1923–2023)</i> was an Italian-American geometer who asked a daring question in <b>1954</b>: <i>'Can a closed, multi-dimensional polynomial space be intricately curved and folded, yet have its gravitational pull balance out to exact zero at every single point (<b>R_μν = 0</b>, like a tension-balanced soap film)?'</i></li><li>For 20 years, most mathematicians believed Calabi's shapes could not exist. Even young geometer <b>Shing-Tung Yau</b> <i>(born 1949)</i> initially thought in 1973 that he had found a counterexample to disprove Calabi! Upon realizing a subtle gap in his counter-argument, Yau reversed course and in <b>1977</b> proved that <b>Calabi was right — these Ricci-flat polynomial worlds truly exist!</b> Yau won the <b>Fields Medal (1982)</b> (the 'Nobel Prize of mathematics'), and the shapes were named <b>Calabi–Yau manifolds</b>.</li></ul>",
    w3_lore_c3:
      "<div class='lore-title'>3. Why Do Physicists Care? Superstrings, 10 Dimensions & the Garden Hose</div>In the 20th century, physics was split in two: Einstein's General Relativity explained gravity and galaxies, while Quantum Mechanics explained atoms and electrons — yet combining their equations produced mathematical nonsense! In <b>1984–1985</b>, physicists discovered that <b>Superstring Theory</b> unites gravity and quantum physics, but only if our Universe has <b>10 dimensions</b> instead of 4!<ul><li><b>Where are the other 6 dimensions hiding?</b> Picture a long garden hose stretched high in the sky. From far away, it looks like a 1D thread. But an ant crawling on the hose sees a second dimension wrapped in a tiny circle around the tube!</li><li>In the exact same way, at <b>every point in your room</b>, 6 microscopic dimensions are curled up into a tiny <b>Calabi–Yau crystal</b> (whose 4D slice at degree <b>n = 5</b> you are rotating on the screen above!).</li></ul>",
    w3_lore_c4:
      "<div class='lore-title'>4. Real-World Applications: Where Does This Math Work Today?</div><ul><li><b>Particle Physics:</b> just as the shape and holes of a flute determine its musical notes, the number of petals and topological holes in a Calabi–Yau polynomial determines how quantum strings vibrate — setting the masses of electrons and quarks!</li><li><b>3D Games, Animation & Spacecraft (SO(4) & Quaternions):</b> the exact 4D sphere <b>S³</b> and Hopf fibration math from this window runs millions of times per second inside <b>Unreal Engine, Unity, Maya, Houdini, and Blender</b> (as 4D quaternions) to rotate 3D cameras and satellites smoothly without <i>Gimbal Lock</i>.</li><li><b>Quantum Computers & Optics:</b> the Hopf fibration maps entangled two-qubit states in quantum processors and shapes optical laser vortices.</li></ul>",

