        b2: "<b>Where to look:</b> Look at the glowing dots inside the circular radar and the readouts <b>z₁, z₂, z₃, z₄</b> <i>('zee-one, zee-two...')</i> below it. By Gauss's <b>Fundamental Theorem of Algebra</b>, a degree-<b>n</b> polynomial ALWAYS has exactly <b>n</b> roots on this radar! Green dots sit on the real X-axis; purple dots have stepped into the complex plane.",
        b3: "<b>How it works live (Durand–Kerner solver):</b> Under the hood, a numerical Durand–Kerner solver computes all complex roots of <b>P(z) = 0</b> on every frame. Slowly sweep <b>a₀</b> in Window #1 from −1 to +1: watch two green roots accelerate toward each other along X, collide head-on at (0,0), and ricochet 90° apart up and down the vertical imaginary axis as conjugate roots <b>±i·y</b>!"
